['Here is an example of a basic logger in C using the POSIX API `syslog` for system error log and simple file I/O with `fopen, fwrite, fclose` for custom logging to file. The maximum number of logs per file is controlled via an external config file (max_logs).

The code below only provides a basic skeleton and does not include integration with existing frameworks or dealing with external data sources as it would require a specific implementation based on your requirements, which isn't specified in this problem.

```c
#include <stdarg.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <syslog.h>

#define MAX_LOGS 100 // can be adjusted via config file
FILE * logfile = NULL;
int current_logs = 0;

void open_log(const char *filename) {
    if (logfile == NULL) {
        logfile = fopen(filename, "a");
        if (logfile == NULL) {
            syslog(LOG_ERR | LOG_USER, "Unable to open log file: %m\n");
            return;
        }
    }
}

void close_log() {
    if (logfile != NULL) {
        fclose(logfile);
        logfile = NULL;
        current_logs = 0;
    }
}

void flush_logs() {
    // Flush to disk or perform other actions based on your requirements
}

void check_flush_logs() {
    if (current_logs >= MAX_LOGS) {
        flush_logs();
        current_logs = 0;
    }
}

void log_message(int priority, const char *format, ...) {
    open_log("/path/to/your/logfile");

    if (logfile == NULL) return; // Failed to open log file

    va_list args;
    va_start(args, format);

    time_t t = time(NULL);
    struct tm *tm = localtime(&t);
    char timestamp[20];
    strftime(timestamp, sizeof(timestamp), "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S", tm);

    // Log to syslog
    vsyslog(priority, format, args);

    // Log to file
    fprintf(logfile, "[%s] ", timestamp);
    vfprintf(logfile, format, args);
    fprintf(logfile, "\n");

    va_end(args);

    current_logs++;
    check_flush_logs();
}
```
This code logs messages to both syslog and a file. The `check_flush_logs` function can be adjusted according to your requirements, such as checking disk space or triggering on a regular interval. 

For integration with an external config file for dynamic logging levels, you would need to parse this file when the program starts up and adjust the priority argument in the `log_message` function accordingly. You would also need some way of notifying your program that the config has changed, such as monitoring the file or having it scan it periodically. 

Note: This code does not handle error checking thoroughly for the sake of brevity and readability. In a real-world scenario, you should always check return values from system calls to ensure they were successful.
']

