['In C, we can use the `dlfcn` library for dynamic loading and linking of libraries. The code below is an example of how you might implement this. However, please note that it does not include all the error handling mechanisms required in a production environment.

The main design tradeoff here is to keep everything simple for simplicity and robustness while still implementing thread safety, caching mechanism and dynamic loading functionality. This also includes not including a full example of an external cryptographic library for this response as it would be quite complex and beyond the scope of this question. 

```c
#include <dlfcn.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

typedef struct {
    char* name;  
    int identifier;  // unique id for each hash function/block cipher
    void* (*hash_function)(const unsigned char *, size_t); // pointer to the implementation function of the algorithm
} hash_or_cipher;

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;  
hash_or_cipher* cached_functions = NULL; 
bool cache_valid = false;

void load_and_cache_algorithms() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // locking for thread safety when accessing the list of algorithms.

    if (cache_valid) { 
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);  
        return;
    }

    void* handle = dlopen("libcrypto.so", RTLD_LAZY); // loading the library dynamically
    if (!handle) {
       printf("Failed to load library: %s\n", dlerror()); 
       pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);  
       return;
    }

    /* ... code for extracting hash_or_cipher from the handle and storing in cached_functions */

    cache_valid = true; // Mark the cache as valid.
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); 
}

hash_or_cipher* get_algorithms() {
    if (!cache_valid) load_and_cache_algorithms();

    return cached_functions;  
}
```
This code doesn't include the code to extract the algorithms from the library, as it would be quite complex and depends on how the library is structured. For this reason I suggest looking into the specific cryptographic libraries you are using for more detailed instructions. 

Please note that this is a simplified version of the requested functionality. In real-world scenarios, error handling should be significantly more robust and include mechanisms to free any memory allocated before returning errors, use `dlerror()` to provide meaningful error messages in case of failures, consider other factors such as endianness compatibility or different versions of libraries, etc.
