["Here is a C++ implementation using `std::visit` to handle the different types of variants, along with `find_if` and `inserter` from the STL for memory efficiency and simplicity.

```cpp
#include <variant>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <stdexcept>

using var = std::variant<int, std::string>;

void insertAfterVariant(std::vector<var>& vec, const var& findVar, const var& inserVar) {
    auto it = std::find_if(vec.begin(), vec.end(), [&](const var& v){ return v == findVar; });

    if (it != vec.end()) {
        vec.insert(++it, inserVar);
    } else {
        vec.push_back(inserVar);
    }
}
```

For error handling, we throw `std::runtime_error` when the input type is different from our expected types. We also need to ensure that this function is not called with variables of other types by wrapping it into a custom struct and using `visit`:

```cpp
struct VariantVector {
    std::vector<var> vec;

    void insertAfterVariant(const var& findVar, const var& inserVar) {
        auto it = std::find_if(vec.begin(), vec.end(), [&](const var& v){ 
            return std::visit([&](auto&& arg1) {
                return std::holds_alternative<decltype(arg1)>(v) && arg1 == std::get<decltype(arg1)>(findVar);
            }, v);
        });

        if (it != vec.end()) {
            vec.insert(++it, inserVar);
        } else {
            vec.push_back(inserVar);
        }
    }
};
```

To handle concurrency, we need to ensure that this function is thread-safe. A simple approach for a single writer (one insertion at a time) could be a `std::mutex`:

```cpp
