['Here is a basic example of how you might implement this functionality in C on Unix systems using fork and execvp to run the command:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <fcntl.h>

// Global variable to keep track of the number of concurrent processes.
static int running_processes = 0;

void handle_signal(int signum) {
    if (signum == SIGINT) {
        // If Ctrl+C is pressed, terminate both child and parent processes.
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    } else {
        // Ignore all other signals during execution of the child process.
        signal(signum, SIG_IGN);
    }
}

int run_command(char *filepath) {
    if (running_processes >= 10) {
        // If maximum number of concurrent processes is reached, return 0.
        fprintf(stderr, "Maximum number of concurrent processes reached.\n");
        return 0;
    }

    pid_t child = fork();

    if (child == -1) {
        perror("fork failed");
        return 0; // fork failed
    } else if (child == 0) {
        signal(SIGINT, handle_signal);

        char *argv[2];
        argv[0] = filepath;
        argv[1] = NULL;

        execvp(filepath, argv); // Execute the command.

        fprintf(stderr, "exec failed: %s\n", strerror(errno));
        return 0; // execvp failed
    } else {
        running_processes++;
        int status;
        waitpid(child, &status, 0); // Wait for the child process to finish.

        if (!WIFEXITED(status) || WEXITSTATUS(status) != 0) {
            return 0; // The command failed or was terminated by a signal.
        } else {
            running_processes--;
            return 1; // The command succeeded.
        }
    }
}
```
This code handles errors for fork and exec, but does not handle any form of configuration loading or logging. It also only logs to stderr and does not support configurable logging levels.
']

['The following is a basic implementation of the described function in C# using `Process` class. This example assumes that adding a site to an IIS server can be handled by running a command line tool like `appcmd.exe` and starting websites can be done with `iisreset`. 

Please note that this is just a simple skeleton, and you may need to adjust it based on your specific requirements or use case. For instance, the way to check if web.config file exists, add site, start site etc could be different in real-world scenarios. Also, error handling should be more detailed based on what operations can fail under which conditions. 

```csharp
using System;
using System.Diagnostics;
using System.IO;
using System.Threading;
using NLog; // An example of a logging framework

public class WebsiteManager
{
    private static readonly Logger Logger = LogManager.GetCurrentClassLogger();
    private static readonly SemaphoreSlim semaphore = new SemaphoreSlim(1, 1);

    public bool AddAndStartSite(string directoryPath, string siteName)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(directoryPath) || string.IsNullOrEmpty(siteName))
            throw new ArgumentException("Directory path and Site name cannot be null or empty");

        semaphore.Wait(); // Lock to avoid race conditions

        try
        {
            if (!File.Exists(Path.Combine(directoryPath, "web.config")))
                return false;

            Logger.Info($"Adding site {siteName} at location {directoryPath}");
            RunCommand("appcmd", $"add site /name:{siteName} /physicalPath:{directoryPath}");

            Logger.Info($"Starting site {siteName}");
            RunCommand("iisreset", "/start");
        }
        finally
        {
            semaphore.Release(); // Release lock so others can use the resource
        }

        return true;
    }

    private void RunCommand(string filename, string arguments)
    {
        var process = new Process()
        {
            StartInfo = new ProcessStartInfo
            {
                FileName = filename,
                Arguments = arguments,
                RedirectStandardOutput = true,
                UseShellExecute = false,
                CreateNoWindow = true,
            }
        };

        process.Start();
        string output = process.StandardOutput.ReadToEnd();
        process.WaitForExit();
    }
}
```
The `AddAndStartSite` function implements the described requirements: it checks if a web.config file exists, adds and starts the site using external command-line tools, handles race conditions by locking with a semaphore, logs operations for debugging/tracking purposes, and gracefully handles exceptions in a basic way (re-throwing them). 

Remember to install NLog via the Package Manager Console: `Install-Package NLog`.
']

['Here is an example of how you might implement this in Java, using Gradle for building, Apache Commons Configurations for managing external configuration files, and SLF4J for logging.

```java
import org.apache.commons.configuration2.*;
import org.apache.commons.configuration2.builder.fluent.Configurations;
import org.apache.commons.configuration2.ex.ConfigurationException;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class CommandRunner {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(CommandRunner.class);
    private static final String COMMAND_KEY = "command";
    private static final File PROPERTIES_FILE = new File("config.properties");

    public void run() {
        PropertiesConfiguration config = loadProperties();

        if (config != null && config.containsKey(COMMAND_KEY)) {
            String command = config.getString(COMMAND_KEY);

            for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
                try {
                    runCommand(command);
                    return;
                } catch (IOException | InterruptedException e) {
                    logger.warn("Failed to execute command, retrying...", e);

                    try {
                        TimeUnit.SECONDS.sleep(1 << i); // Exponential backoff
                    } catch (InterruptedException ex) {
                        Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                        return;
                    }
                }
            }

            logger.error("Failed to execute command after 3 attempts.");
        } else {
            logger.error("Command not found in configuration file");
        }
    }

    private PropertiesConfiguration loadProperties() {
        Configurations configs = new Configurations();

        try {
            return configs.properties(PROPERTIES_FILE);
        } catch (ConfigurationException e) {
            logger.error("Failed to load configuration file", e);
            return null;
        }
    }

    private void runCommand(String command) throws IOException, InterruptedException {
        Process process = Runtime.getRuntime().exec(command);

        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(process.getInputStream()))) {
            String line;

            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                logger.info("Output: {}", line);
            }
        }

        try (BufferedReader errorReader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(process.getErrorStream()))) {
            String error;

            while ((error = errorReader.readLine()) != null) {
                logger.info("Output: {}", error);
            }
        }

        process.waitFor();
    }
}
```
To handle file deletion, you might want to implement a mechanism that moves files to a temporary location instead of deleting them directly, and then cleaning up the temporary directory at regular intervals or when certain conditions are met (e.g., no active processes using the files).

This is just a basic example - for a production environment, there would be more considerations such as security measures to protect sensitive data in properties file, error handling and logging improvements etc.

In terms of documentation, I'd suggest adding Javadocs to methods and classes with descriptions that make sense within the context of your application. Also, create a README file or similar document that details how to configure and use the properties file, including instructions on setting up SSL for secure communication if necessary. Make sure to communicate any trade-offs you made in the code and why they were necessary or beneficial.
']

