```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct FSA {
    int state;
    // add other fields as necessary...
} FSA;

typedef struct StateList {
    int state;
    struct StateList* next;
} StateList;

// Global mutex for thread safety
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

StateList* createNewStateList(int* states, int numStates, FSA* fsa, int lastState) {
    StateList *list = NULL, *tail;

    // Acquire lock to ensure thread safety while accessing shared data
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    for (int i = 0; i < numStates; ++i) {
        if (fsa->state == states[i]) { // Replace with actual state transition logic
            StateList* newNode = malloc(sizeof(StateList));

            // Check if memory allocation was successful
            if (!newNode) {
                pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); 
                return NULL;
            }

            newNode->state = states[i];
            newNode->next = NULL;

            if (list == NULL) { // First node in list
                list = newNode;
            } else { 
                tail->next = newNode;
            }

            tail = newNode;
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    return list;
}
```

In this example, I've used a simple linked list to represent the state list. This could be replaced with a more efficient data structure depending on your requirements and use case. 

The `createNewStateList` function accepts an array of states, the number of states, a pointer to the FSA, and the last state in the list. It traverses through the given states and checks if they exist in the FSA. If a match is found, it creates a new node for this state and adds it to the end of the list.

The list of nodes is stored as a linked list where each `StateList` struct has a pointer to its successor ('next'). 

