['Here's a basic implementation in Java. It doesn't take into account all the specifics you mentioned but it should give you a starting point:

```java
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.*;
import org.apache.logging.log4j.LogManager;
import org.apache.logging.log4j.Logger;

public class HashUtil {
    private static Logger logger = LogManager.getRootLogger();
    private static Map<String, String> cache = Collections.synchronizedMap(new LinkedHashMap<>()); // For LRU caching
    private static int MAX_CACHE_SIZE = 10000;

    public static synchronized String getMD5Hash(String input) throws Exception {
        if (input == null || input.isEmpty()) {
            logger.error("Input string is invalid");
            throw new InvalidInputException("Input string cannot be empty or null");
        } 

        // Check cache first
        String cachedValue = cache.get(input);
        if (cachedValue != null) {
            return cachedValue;
        } 

        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
        byte[] digest = md.digest(input.getBytes());
        String hash = Arrays.toString(digest);

        // If cache is full, remove the oldest element (LRU)
        if (cache.size() >= MAX_CACHE_SIZE) {
            cache.remove(cache.keySet().iterator().next());
        } 

        cache.put(input, hash);
        return hash;
    }

    public static class InvalidInputException extends Exception {
        public InvalidInputException(String errorMessage) {
            super(errorMessage);
        }
    }
}
```
This code uses the `java.security` library to generate MD5 hashes, and `Log4j2` for logging. It implements an LRU cache with a maximum size using a synchronized map. 

The hash of the input string is first checked in the cache. If it's not found there, it will be computed and added to the cache. If the cache is full after adding a new element, the oldest (least recently used) entry is removed to maintain space for the new one.

Please note that this code does not include any external data source integration or any LFU or other caching strategies, as implementing these would significantly increase complexity and might not be necessary depending on your specific use case. Always consider trade-offs when designing software solutions. 

Also remember to handle exceptions in your main methods where you call this `HashUtil` method appropriately. In the provided code, it throws an unchecked exception `InvalidInputException` for invalid input string which should ideally be handled by the calling method.
']

