['The function `loadCipherModule` is quite complex as it involves working with external data sources, system dependencies, error handling and memory management. The following code uses the OpenSSL library to dynamically load a specified cipher module at runtime and allows configuration via command line options. This implementation does not include an existing logging framework integration.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <openssl/evp.h>
#include <dlfcn.h>

typedef struct {
    void *handle;
    const EVP_CIPHER *(*EVP_get_cipherbyname)(const char *);
} CipherObject;

CipherObject* loadCipherModule(const char* moduleName, const char* options) {
    void *handle;
    const EVP_CIPHER *(*EVP_get_cipherbyname)(const char *);
    const EVP_CIPHER *(*ssl_get_cipher)(void);
    CipherObject *module = NULL;

    handle = dlopen(moduleName, RTLD_NOW | RTLD_GLOBAL);
    if (!handle) {
        fprintf(stderr, "dlopen failed: %s\n", dlerror());
        return NULL;
    }

    EVP_get_cipherbyname = (void*)(intptr_t)dlsym(handle, "EVP_get_cipherbyname");
    if (!EVP_get_cipherbyname) {
        fprintf(stderr, "dlsym failed: %s\n", dlerror());
        return NULL;
    }

    module = malloc(sizeof(*module));
    if (module == NULL) {
        perror("malloc");
        return NULL;
    }

    module->handle = handle;
    module->EVP_get_cipherbyname = EVP_get_cipherbyname;

    // Apply options
    if (options != NULL) {
        ssl_get_cipher = module->EVP_get_cipherbyname(options);
        if (!ssl_get_cipher) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Applying options failed\n");
            free(module);
            return NULL;
        }
    }

    // Handle system dependencies and external data sources here...

    return module;
}
```
This function loads the cipher module specified by `moduleName` using dlopen(). It then gets a pointer to the EVP_get_cipherbyname() function within that module. If either of these operations fails, it prints an error message and returns NULL. A CipherObject is created to hold the handle to the loaded library and the EVP_get_cipherbyname function.

If `options` are provided, they are applied by calling EVP_get_cipherbyname() with these options as arguments. If this fails, an error message is printed and NULL is returned. It's important to note that applying options for ciphers is highly dependent on the specific module being loaded.

Lastly, any system dependencies or external data sources should be handled at this point. This has not been implemented in the given code snippet as it would need more context about what these entail and how to implement them correctly. It's recommended to handle such tasks with care to prevent errors and memory leaks.
