response:
['The problem can be solved by maintaining a count of the total memory allocated and checking this against the limit each time memory is allocated or reallocated. We also need to make sure we're not returning invalid pointers from our functions, particularly when dealing with multi-threaded applications. 

In the following code, `malloc_guard` and `realloc_guard` are macros that help guard against unchecked allocation and reallocation respectively. The dynamic memory function `allocateMemory` is used for allocating memory safely and checking whether we have exceeded our limit. For initialization, we've added a new function `initializeData` which initializes the arrays with random values. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct record {
    int value;
} record;

static size_t totalMemory = 0;
static pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

#define malloc_guard(ptr, size) \
    do { \
        if ((ptr = malloc(size)) == NULL) { \
            fprintf(stderr, "Memory allocation failed.\n"); \
            exit(1); \
        } \
    } while (0)

#define realloc_guard(ptr, size) \
    do { \
        void *tmp = realloc(ptr, size); \
        if (tmp == NULL && size != 0) { \
            fprintf(stderr, "Memory reallocation failed.\n"); \
            exit(1); \
        } else { \
            ptr = tmp; \
        } \
    } while (0)

void* allocateMemory(size_t size) {
    void *ptr = NULL;
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    if ((totalMemory + size) > limit) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Exceeded memory limit.\n");
        exit(1);
    } else {
        totalMemory += size;
        malloc_guard(ptr, size);
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return ptr;
}

void initializeData(long* offsets, int* keys, record* records, int size) {
    for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
        offsets[i] = rand() % 100;
        keys[i] = rand() % 100;
        records[i].value = rand() % 100;
    }
}

void dynamicAllocateAndInitialize(long** offsets, int** keys, record** records, int size) {
    *offsets = allocateMemory(size * sizeof(long));
    *keys = allocateMemory(size * sizeof(int));
    *records = allocateMemory(size * sizeof(record));

    initializeData(*offsets, *keys, *records, size);
}
```
In this code, we use a global mutex lock to ensure thread safety. It's worth noting that in multithreaded applications, you should consider using thread-safe data structures or libraries for memory management which handle the synchronization for you. 

Please replace `limit` with your actual limit. This function assumes that all allocations and reallocations will be successful, which may not always be the case. In a real-world scenario, proper error checking needs to be in place. 

The memory usage tracking is kept simple for demonstration purposes but you might want to use something more sophisticated depending on your specific needs (e.g., keep track of memory usage per thread). The initialization function assumes that all arrays will have the same size as the allocated memory blocks, which may not always be the case in a real-world scenario.
']

