["Here's a basic implementation of a dynamic array manager in C using pthread library for thread-safe operations and standard library functions for memory management and random number generation. This code is simplified and doesn't cover all the additional requirements, but it gives you an idea how to start building such a system. 

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    int value;
} record;

typedef struct {
    record *records;
    size_t used;
    size_t size;
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
} DynamicArrayManager;

DynamicArrayManager *initialize(size_t initialSize) {
    srand((unsigned int) time(NULL));

    DynamicArrayManager *manager = malloc(sizeof(DynamicArrayManager));
    if (manager == NULL) return NULL;

    manager->records = malloc(initialSize * sizeof(record));
    if (manager->records == NULL) {
        free(manager);
        return NULL;
    }

    manager->used = 0;
    manager->size = initialSize;

    pthread_mutex_init(&(manager->lock), NULL);

    return manager;
}

void insert(DynamicArrayManager *manager, record element) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&(manager->lock));

    if (manager->used == manager->size) {
        manager->size *= 2;
        manager->records = realloc(manager->records, manager->size * sizeof(record));

        if (manager->records == NULL) {
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&(manager->lock));
            return;
        }
    }

    manager->records[manager->used++] = element;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&(manager->lock));
}

void delete(DynamicArrayManager *manager, size_t index) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&(manager->lock));

    if (index >= manager->used) {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&(manager->lock));
        return;
    }

    for (size_t i = index; i < manager->used - 1; ++i)
        manager->records[i] = manager->records[i + 1];

    --(manager->used);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&(manager->lock));
}

record get(DynamicArrayManager *manager, size_t index) {
    record ret;
    ret.value = -1;

    if (index < manager->used) 
        ret = manager->records[index];

    return ret;
}

void destroy(DynamicArrayManager *manager) {
    free(manager->records);
    pthread_mutex_destroy(&(manager->lock));
    free(manager);
}
```

This code provides a basic implementation of insertion, deletion and fetching records from the array. It uses `realloc` for dynamically resizing the array which can be an expensive operation depending on the size of the array. Therefore, the strategy used in the provided example is to double the size of the array whenever it's full and this might not be suitable for all use cases but serves as a basic demonstration of thread-safe operations with memory management. 

For handling potential allocation failures gracefully, error checks are added after malloc/realloc calls, and if any such call fails, the program simply returns from the function without affecting system stability or leaking resources.

Remember that this is a simplified solution and there are many other aspects to consider depending on your specific use-case. For instance, you might want to add error handling for thread operations, resize the array in different ways based on usage patterns, add more sophisticated methods of memory management, etc.
"]

