['Here's a basic implementation of what you're asking for in C. This program assumes that the CSV file is formatted with no missing values and each line contains exactly three fields, separated by commas. It also assumes that there are no empty lines in the CSV file, as it directly reads three strings from each line:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct sensor_data {
    double timestamp;
    char* name;
    double reading;
} sensor_data;

sensor_data* parse_csv(const char* filename, int *num_lines) {
    FILE* file = fopen(filename, "r");
    if (file == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to open %s\n", filename);
        return NULL;
    }

    sensor_data* data = malloc(sizeof(sensor_data));
    *num_lines = 0;

    char line[256];
    while (fgets(line, sizeof(line), file)) {
        (*num_lines)++;

        // Resize array to hold new data
        data = realloc(data, sizeof(sensor_data) * (*num_lines));
        if (data == NULL) {
            printf("Failed to allocate memory\n");
            return NULL;
        }

        char* timestampStr = strtok(line, ",");
        char* sensorNameStr = strtok(NULL, ",");
        char* readingStr = strtok(NULL, ",");

        // Check if all fields were read from the line
        if (timestampStr == NULL || sensorNameStr == NULL || readingStr == NULL) {
            printf("Invalid data format in file: %s\n", filename);
            continue;
        }

        // Parse and store each field in the struct
        char* endPtr;
        double timestamp = strtod(timestampStr, &endPtr);
        if (endPtr == timestampStr || *endPtr != '\0') {
            printf("Invalid timestamp: %s\n", timestampStr);
            continue;
        }

        char* sensorName = malloc(strlen(sensorNameStr) + 1);
        strcpy(sensorName, sensorNameStr);

        double reading = strtod(readingStr, &endPtr);
        if (endPtr == readingStr || *endPtr != '\0') {
            printf("Invalid reading: %s\n", readingStr);
            free(sensorName);
            continue;
        }

        data[(*num_lines) - 1].timestamp = timestamp;
        data[(*num_lines) - 1].name = sensorName;
        data[(*num_lines) - 1].reading = reading;
    }

    fclose(file);
    return data;
}
```

This function dynamically allocates memory for the `sensor_data` structures as they are read from the CSV file. It checks that each line of the CSV file contains exactly three fields, skipping any lines that do not meet this requirement. It also logs any invalid data it encounters and continues to process valid data.

Note: This is a simple example and does not cover all possible edge cases or error conditions. In practice, you would want to include more robust error handling (e.g., checking the return values of `fopen`, `strtod`, etc.) and potentially use a more complex method for parsing CSV files that can handle different character encodings and missing fields in each line.
']

['Sure, here is an example implementation in C using malloc() function for dynamic memory allocation, srand() function for random seeding, rand() function for generating random numbers, and free() function for deallocation of allocated memory when it's no longer needed to prevent memory leaks.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct Record {
    int value;
} Record;

int generate_random(int min, int max) {
    return (rand() % (max - min + 1)) + min;
}

void logErrorAndExit(const char* message) {
    printf("%s", message);
    exit(0);
}

Record** initArrayOfRecords(long *offsets, int *keys, size_t arraySize, int minVal, int maxVal) {
    if (arraySize <= 0) logErrorAndExit("Invalid Array Size");

    srand((unsigned int) time(NULL)); // seeding rand() function with the current time

    Record **records = malloc(sizeof(Record*) * arraySize);
    if (!records) logErrorAndExit("Memory Allocation Error for Records Array");

    for (int i = 0; i < arraySize; ++i) {
        records[i] = malloc(sizeof(Record));
        if(!records[i]) logErrorAndExit("Memory Allocation Error for a Record");

        offsets[i] = (long)(records + i); // storing memory address as an offset
        keys[i] = generate_random(minVal, maxVal); // generating random key
        records[i]->value = generate_random(minVal, maxVal); // generating random value in the record
    }

    return records;
}

void freeArrayOfRecords(Record **records, size_t arraySize) {
    for (int i = 0; i < arraySize; ++i) {
        free(records[i]);
    }

    free(records);
}
```

This code defines a struct named Record with one int field. It also includes a function generate_random() to generate random numbers within the specified range, and logErrorAndExit() for error handling when memory allocation fails. 

The main part of the code is initArrayOfRecords(), which allocates memory dynamically for an array of records and generates random values for keys and record's int field. It also handles possible errors during memory allocation by checking if each malloc() call returned a valid pointer, or else it exits the program with an error message. 

